\description{
\code{qes_spec()} is the one entry point to the harmonization specification: the
reviewed rules that say, study by study, which question feeds each
harmonized variable ("target") of \code{\link[=qes_harmonize]{qes_harmonize()}}, how its codes map to
the target's levels, why each missing value is missing and how comparable
each study's question is to the target's anchor question.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{view = "targets"} (default): one row per target, with its definition
and levels, and one column per study giving the best comparability grade
of that study's question (\code{NA}: no question for the target).
\item \code{view = "crosswalk"}: one row per crosswalk row (\code{level = "row"}): the
source variable, grade and its reason, instrument, offered levels and
the levels not offered (structural zeros), filter question, wording (or
the document that gives it), recommended weight and review status. With
\code{level = "code"}, one row per source code, with the level or NA reason it
maps to; \code{format = "retroharmonize"} gives that table under the column
names of the retroharmonize package's crosswalk tables. Printing the
view of a single target shows its section of the generated reference.
\item \code{view = "pooled"}: one row per member of each pooled variable (such as
\code{vote_choice}, which pools the reported vote and the vote intentions):
its type name (the value of \verb{<pooled>__type}), precedence (the member
tried first is 1), whether it is used by default, the transform of its
values and its grade cap, and one column per study giving the member's
grade there (capped; \code{NA}: no question). \code{targets} selects pooled
variables (or the set \code{"pooled"}).
\item \code{view = "relaxed"}: one row per column of \code{\link[=qes_decon]{qes_decon()}}, the relaxed
harmonization: its label, levels, base (the strict target or pooled
variable it reuses, if any), the transform of the base's values, how it
was relaxed, and one column per study saying where the study's values
come from (\code{"strict"}: the base; \code{"relaxed"}: a relaxed mapping of the
study's own question, with its status when it is not signed off yet;
\code{NA}: none; a column built on another column takes that column's
source). \code{targets} selects columns.
\item \code{view = "relaxed_maps"}: one row per relaxed mapping (study, wave and
column): the source variable, rule, value map, gate, whether it
replaces the base, the recode in words, the wording or the document
that gives it, notes and review status. \code{targets} selects columns.
\item \code{view = "spec"}: the spec itself, as an object of class \code{qes_spec}
(tables, version and content hash), checked by the validator and the
data checks; this is what \verb{spec =} of \code{\link[=qes_harmonize]{qes_harmonize()}} accepts.
}

The spec is loaded and checked once per session. The shipped spec passes
every check; a spec directory of your own is checked the same way.
}
\section{Experimental}{

The spec is experimental: targets, grades and mappings are reviewed study
by study and may change. Its version and content hash are recorded in every
result of \code{\link[=qes_harmonize]{qes_harmonize()}}.
}

\section{En français}{

\code{qes_spec()} (expérimental) montre la spécification d'harmonisation : la
vue \code{"targets"} donne une ligne par cible et, pour chaque étude, son niveau
de comparabilité ; la vue \code{"crosswalk"} donne la question source, la
raison du niveau, les niveaux offerts et non offerts, la question filtre et
le libellé (une vague \code{"*"} désigne une ligne qui s'applique à chaque
sondage des sondages CROP regroupés) ; la vue \code{"spec"} renvoie la
spécification vérifiée, dont la table \code{tables$legacy}, qui rend les
colonnes de \code{\link[=get_qes_master]{get_qes_master()}} et de \code{\link[=get_decon]{get_decon()}} à partir des cibles.
L'ensemble de cibles \code{"decon"} réunit les cibles des colonnes de
\code{get_decon()}. La vue \code{"pooled"} décrit les variables regroupées (comme
\code{vote_choice}, qui réunit le vote déclaré et les intentions de vote) :
leurs membres, leur ordre de priorité et le niveau de chaque membre dans
chaque étude. \code{lang = "fr"} donne les étiquettes, définitions et raisons en français.
Les vues \code{"relaxed"} et \code{"relaxed_maps"} décrivent l'harmonisation
souple de \code{\link[=qes_decon]{qes_decon()}} : ses colonnes, leur base et la façon dont elles
sont assouplies, puis l'appariement souple propre à chaque étude.
\code{vignette("fr-reference-harmonisation", package = "qesR")} en est la
référence complète.
}

